    ogc:resolves rul:C-06 ;
    ogc:rulingText "IRIs are authored under https://w3id.org/og-caie/ from the start. The GitHub Pages URL is noted as the fallback until the w3id redirect is merged." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Author against w3id now, note the Pages fallback" .

rul:R-11 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "No assessor-of-assessors parts or shapes. The conclusion closes with a call to action: use OG-CAIE, or have an AI evaluation practice audited against this specification." ;
    ogc:order 11 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-12 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Technical experts may in principle have mirrored counterparts who assess the assessment, but this document does not model assessing the assessors. The one plausible place for the idea is a closing call to action, offering the reader OG-CAIE or an audit of the reader's own AI evaluation practice against this specification." ;
    ogc:verbatim "in principle they may have mirrored counterparts who assess the assessment but for the purpose of this document i don't think we want to get into assessing the assessors. except plausible as a final call to action offering the reader OG-CAIE or an audit of the readers AI eval practice against our OG-CAIE spec." .

rul:R-24 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Every step of the EPO action def now declares its inputs as well as its outputs. Delivery is an item kind of its own (the report and the recommendation handed to the sponsor), the output of the account executive's delivery activity, carried on DeliveryWrite through a delivery seam to the sponsor and a second seam into the record; the sponsor's report and recommendation ports and the executive's reuse of RecommendationWrite and ReportWrite are gone. Shape M5-PortsBelongToRoles now restricts the account executive to AgreementWrite and DeliveryWrite. The one activity two actor categories may both perform is determining on evidence (R-21), so DeterminationWrite is the only supplier port definition carried by two roles, and the wiring test says exactly that." ;
    ogc:order 24 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-27 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Port sharing is bad practice: it confuses activities with actors. Actors may perform multiple activities, but each activity needs precise inputs and outputs." ;
    ogc:verbatim "port sharing seems like bad practice to me. i think you are confusing activities with actors. actors can perform multiple activities but the activities need precise inputs and outputs" .

rul:R-27 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Z captured the OBP page for clauses 3.3.9 to 3.3.13 (screenshot 38, hash in sources/sources.ttl): the 2026 wording of contract is the 2015 wording, binding agreement. The quote is verified by Z on 2026-09-06; sheet 03 row 7 ticked." ;
    ogc:order 27 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-24 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The ISO 9000:2026 clause for contract is 3.3.13. The page carrying clauses 3.3.9 to 3.3.13 is captured and the quote, binding agreement, is verified; sheet 03 row 7 is ticked." ;
    ogc:verbatim "contract is 3.3.13" .

rul:R-30 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "The test now bans only the name (Popper, Popperian) and falsifiability's forms from the inner pages; hypothesis, prediction, auxiliary and evidence are free words there, and the rule about their sense is stated in CLAUDE.md and read, not grepped. The two scrubbed sentences on the record page stay scrubbed: they used the words in Popper's sense." ;
    ogc:order 30 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-33 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Care is needed with the Popperian terms because they are common English words. The restriction is not on using the words but on using them in Popper's sense with Popper's definitions. The discussion of what is science, grounded in Popper, stays in the bookends, since it is the paper's core argument: \"CAIE would be science if it covers these things\", ending with \"OG-CAIE demonstrably covers these things\". The inner sections achieve this through engineering standards. The introduction and conclusion cover why and what; the inner chapters cover what and how in enough detail, and executably, to show both in theory through the executable specification and in practice through the worked example that the bar for science is met." ;
    ogc:verbatim "need to be careful with the popper terms because they are pretty common english words. the restriction isn't on using the word its on using them in Popper sense with the popper definitions. that discussion of what is science, grounded in popper stays in the bookends since its essentially the papers core argument: \"CAIE would be science if it covers these things\" and ends with demonstrably \"OG-CAIE\" covers these things. but the inner sections are achieving this via engineering standards. think of the intro and conclusion as covering \"why/what\" and the inner chapters covering \"what/how\" in enough detail (and executable) so we can show both in theory using our executable spec and in practice through the worked example that we meet the bar for science." .

rul:R-34 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "The plan of record (2026-09-06) states what the representation must hold and prove and what each of eight pages shows and is rendered from: Why this counts as science, The vocabulary, Contracting, The evaluation, The nested model, What the record proves (executed live in the build), Rulings, Conclusion. Every chapter from Contracting on follows five titled blocks in order (What the standards say; The specification; The walkthrough; Checked; There is more in the model), enforced by a test, with a per-page prose budget. Slice B lands the Contracting chapter: renderer slices by item kind and by step, page tags on the essentials, and rulings sheet 05, generated from the model graph, for Z's block-by-block and wire-by-wire validation (C-30)." ;
    ogc:order 34 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-37 ;
    ogc:rulingText """The site is replanned. The MyST site is a presentation layer on top of a rich model that documents itself in RDF. The glossary records the terms, their definitions, their lineages, their cross-relationships and the judgement calls that led to the choice of those definitions. The SysML model covers the actual processes, which are bound to the standards and literature they implement; the processes encoded become executable, so their properties become verifiable. The worked examples demonstrably conform to those processes and produce data that meets the specifications for the wires in the model. The formal properties can be checked (correct wiring, and any rule encoded as SHACL shape conformance), and the examples walk through to make it concrete and intuitive for a human reader. Each page of the MyST site is therefore a curated view of the underlying model. The updated outline delivers a story arc that uses the Popper bookends to make this intuitive for a general audience, then inside uses the formal engineering standards for contracting and for quality evaluation processes as a nested SysML model, rendered as RDF and enriched with the glossary content to ensure citation traceability; ultimately guaranteeing an auditable record of the AI evaluation done using OG-CAIE, by encoding and enforcing a DSO-conditioned EPO, and concluding that this record meets Popper's bar for being scientific. The task builds out what the model (representation) and the site (presentation) each must do. The separation of representation and presentation is one of the main value propositions of doing this with GitHub, MyST, Jupyter, Python, OpenSysML, rdflib, pySHACL and other executable systems engineering tools, which allow text-based standards to be codified in a formal record with machine-provable traceability in both directions and coverage.

Eight pages, as listed; each page includes both the formal abstract specification and the relevant concrete example, the two running in parallel throughout, with a recognisable pattern so that the reader can find them. There is always more: highlighting the separation principle makes clear that the presentation layer (the site) is a view rather than the model itself (the repository).

The presentation is calibrated for a human reader, so the texture and the volume of the content are tuned to human sensibilities. The representation layer is made further accessible to AI through CLI tools and skills, as was done for the glossary. In practice the views are tailored to carry the most important information for the human reader, and the concrete examples are made didactic relative to the abstract concepts; the two always appear as a pair.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim "i am thinking its a good time to replan the site. one way to think about it, is that the myst site is just a presentation layer on top of a rich model which documents itself via rdf. the glossary painstakingly records our terms, their definitions, their lineages, their cross relationships and and the judgement calls that lead to the choice of these definitions. the sysml model covers the actually processes which are also painstakingly bound to the standards and literature they implement. the processes we're encoding become executable so their properties become verifiable. our worked examples demonstrably conform to those processes and produce data that meets the specs for the wires in the model. the formal properties can be checked (eg correct wiring / any rule encoded as shacl shape conformance can be checked) AND the examples walk through to make it concrete and ituitive for a human reader. thus each page in the myst site therefore is a curated view of the underlying model. i want you to plan an updated outline for delivering a story arc that balances that uses the Popper bookends to make this intuitive for a general audience, then inside uses the formal engineering standards for contracting and for quality evaluation processes as a nested sysml model, rendered as RDF and enriched with the glossary content to ensure citation traceability. ultimately guaranteeing that we can produce an auditable record of the AI eval done using OG-CAIE (by encoding and enforcing a DSO conditioned EPO), and that this record meets Poppers bar for being scientific (conclusion). this task is essentially to build out what our model (representation) and site (presentation) each must do. the seperation of representation and presentation is one of the main value propositions of doing this via github with myst, jupyter, python, opensysml, rdflib, pyshacl and other executable systems engineering tools that allow us to codify the text based standards in a formal record with machine provable traceability (both directions) and coverage. / yes, 8 pages as listed but make sure pages include both formal abstract specs and the relevant concrete examples. we can think of the formal spec and the example demonstating it as running parallel throughout. it makes sense to establish some patterning so this is easy to recognize for the reader. furthermore, there is always \"more\" by highlighting the separation principle we can be clear that the presentation layer (the site) is a view rather than the model itself (the repo). / looks very good. just adding that the presentation is calibrated for a human reader so the texture of the content and the volume of the content must be tuned to human sensibilities. the representation layer can be made further accesable to AI via cli tools and skills like we did with the glossary. in practice this means that we need to tailor our views to include the most important information to the human reader, and making concrete examples didactic relative to the abstract concepts. they should always appear as a pair." .

rul:R-35 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Narration only: a paragraph in the Contracting chapter's walkthrough block, after the record rows, marked as observed in practice and deliberately outside the lifecycle; no step, item or shape added. The standards' word for the repeat, surveillance (ISO/IEC 17000 8.1), is quoted and cited as a seeAlso on the contracting lifecycle class, pending Z's tick on sheet 04 row 12." ;
    ogc:order 35 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-38 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The walkthrough may describe two further steps that are explicitly not part of the contract lifecycle and are identified instead as observed in practice: the county health office tells the chatbot provider to implement changes that result in demonstrable behaviour change per the findings of Humane Intelligence, and the chatbot provider implements the changes, at which point the county health office may require new testing to certify that the flagged issues were addressed. This is not claimed to be in the contracting loop of the standard contracting process; it is an opportunity to show how the evaluation results in demonstrable system behaviour change. For safety-critical deployments these evaluations would be expected to be paired with a release. Since the sponsor organization and the chatbot provider are both already in the example, this is primarily narration, to help the reader understand what the process is like in practice and why it is not an academic exercise." ;
    ogc:verbatim """our walk through can describe an extra step 2 steps which are explicitly not part of the contract lifecycle but should instead be identified as observed in practice:

* the county health office tells the chatbot provider they need to implement changes that result in demonstrable behavior change per the findings of Humane Intelligence and the chatbot provider implements changes (at which point the county heath office may require new testing to certify the flagged issues were addressed).


note that we are not trying to say this is in the contracting loop per the standard contracting process but it is an opportunity to identify how this results in demonstrable system behavior change. for safety critical deployments we'd expect these evaluations to be paired with an release. importantly we already have both the sponsor org and the chatbot provider in our example so this is primarily narration feedback to help the reader understand what this is like in real life and why its not just an academic exercise""" .

rul:R-38 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Model: connection def Obligation (sponsor end, population end) and the assembly's connection obligation from sponsor to affected; the Mission item owns ref part regards : AffectedPopulation[1..*], which the record's epo:regards binds to; derived ogm:relatesFrom and ogm:relatesTo; shape M1-Obligation with the counterexample no-obligation; budget 5500. Views: registry ogc/views.py (layers, assemblage, contracting, evaluation), each with a title, what it brings into focus and what it leaves out; wires between the same two parts braid into one bundle labelled by the item kinds in the order the process produces them; relations dotted; the site's figures render from the registry with the perspective as caption; ogc views and ogc view <name>; skill updated; sheet 05 gains a Relations table." ;
    ogc:order 38 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-41 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Some connectivity should show how the mission of the sponsor organization relates to the affected population; it may be covered in the data but not in the view. Diagrams tend to be overloaded, so in practice they must stay simple, even if some wires are braided into bundles, since the information flows together in practice. Diagrams are important views for humans, so their density must be balanced against the point they make. As long as the code that produces a diagram reads from the model itself and the perspective the view encodes is documented (what it brings into focus and what it leaves out), any view that is compelling narratively is permitted. The view code is hardened by building reusable views and committing the methods with a CLI and skills for their use, which makes other views possible even though the presentation layer curates." ;
    ogc:verbatim "i still think there should be some kind of connectivity showing how the mission of the sponsor org relates to the affected population, maybe that's covered in the data just not in the view. i know the diagramming is tricky because we tend to overload our diagrams. in practice we need to keep them simple even if it means some wires a braided up into bundles since the information flows together in practice. the diagrams a pretty important views for humans so we need to balance their density with the point they are making. as long as the code that produces the diagram reads from the the model itself and we document the perspective the view encodes (what it brings into focus and what it leaves out) then i think we can do whatever views are compeling narrative wise. plus we can always harden the view code by building resuable views and committing the methods with cli and skills for their use. this makes other views possible even though our presentation layer curates" .

rul:R-39 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "An appendix page with a d3 knowledge graph explorer rendered deterministically from the RDF (vocabulary, sources, rulings, essentials, shapes, crosswalk, process, model graph, measles record), each view with its perspective, every node naming the ogc command that prints it; served as static files next to the site; block 5 of every chapter links to it. Built on the branch explorer-appendix, merged after the gate." ;
    ogc:order 39 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-42 ;
    ogc:rulingText "A knowledge graph explorer is added as an appendix, modelled after the authors' earlier d3 work in several repositories. Since the text keeps saying there is more, it is easier to cut the text and link to the explorer. Given the extent of the OWL, SHACL and SPARQL work and the existing machine navigation tools, the human-facing graph exploration interface belongs in the appendix; it must back onto the RDF model (Oxigraph may be used for speed). The work may proceed in parallel with the main body. The appendix is an alternative user interface, not new content." ;
    ogc:verbatim "one other idea i have which i think is interesting would be to put a knowledge graph explorer as an appendix. this would be modeled after work we've done in d3 for several other repos. since we keep saying \"there is more\" it might be easiest to cut down on text and just provide links to the knowledge graph explorer. you have variants in commander, in dsg-ant-private and likely other repos. since we're doing lots of owl, shacl sparql work and we already have the machine navigation tools, it would be good to create the human facing graph exploration interface as the appendix. noting this should backup to the rdf model (you can use oxigraph if it helps with speed). ideally you can get this going in parallel with subagents so we can keep working on the main body. the nice thing about this approach to the appendix is that its an alternative user interface not new content" .

rul:R-41 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "docs/rulings.md becomes Appendix C, last in the toc after Appendix B, framed as Z says: the subjective choices the model is grounded in, implicit in the computational proofs, held as the auxiliary assumptions of the claim to science. The main path is seven pages; the vocabulary page's map and the README say so." ;
    ogc:order 41 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-47 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The rulings move to an appendix. The concept is important, but most readers will not need it. The rulings are already part of the knowledge graph explorer; they fit best as Appendix C. The knowledge graph explorer allows navigation of the whole model and the computational proofs demonstrate the guarantees the model can prove computationally; Appendix C is the acknowledgement of the subjective choices, judgements and interpretations the model is grounded in. These subjectivities are implicit in the objective results the computational proofs produce, and the claim that this is scientific holds with these judgements as auxiliary assumptions. Appendix C is framed as the specification holding itself to its own definition of science." ;
    ogc:verbatim "i think i want ruling moved to an appendix. it is an important concept but the truth is most readers won't care. the rulings are already part of the knowledge graph explorer but i think they fit better as appendix C. since the knowledge graph explorer is allowing navigation of the whole model. the computational proofs demonstrate the guarantees or model can actually prove computationally, this would make appendix C the acknowledgement of the subjective choices, judgements and interpretations that model is grounded in. these subjectivities are implicit in the objective results produced by the computational proofs, and the claim that this is scientific holds with these judgements as auxilliary assumptions. we can frame appendix C as holding ourselves to our own definition of science." .

rul:R-42 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Page titles renamed as Z lists them, in the site's sentence case; file names and links unchanged. The nested model keeps its name." ;
    ogc:order 42 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-48 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The page names are tightened: Glossary becomes Standards and Definitions; Contracting becomes Stakeholders and Contracting; The evaluation becomes Context and Evaluation; What the record proves becomes Records and Reporting; Conclusion stays. This gives a reader who skims the outline a much clearer picture of what the site contains. The appendices provide the first rung of backup and the repository as a whole an even richer one, so the reader need not take the authors on trust: the reader can verify." ;
    ogc:verbatim "we can also tighten up naming: Glossary -> Standards and Definitions; Contracting -> Stakeholders and Contracting; The evaluation -> Context and Evaluation; what the record proves -> Records and Reporting; Conclusion. i think this gives a reader who skims the outline a much clearer picture of the website contains. the appendices provide the first rung of backup and the repo as a whole provides an even richer backup. so the reader doesn't need to just trust us. they can verify themselves" .

rul:R-43 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Appendix D, toolchain and reproducibility, after Appendix C: rendered from the lock file, the pinned converter digests, the gate script and the CI workflow, never typed by hand; a reviewer recipe modeled on the SciPy proceedings supplementary material (uv sync, the gate, ogc doctor, the site)." ;
    ogc:order 43 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-49 ;
    ogc:rulingText "An Appendix D, Toolchain and Reproducibility, is added: a detailed review of the toolchain from virtual environments to the packages used, modelled after the portion of the SciPy proceedings supplementary material that covers the same subject. The reader should see that this is a computational paradigm, not just another document." ;
    ogc:verbatim "and i think we could even add an Appendix D which provides a detailed review of our toolchain from virtual environments to packages we used. \"Appendix D: Toolchain and Reproducibility\" modeled after the portion of the scipy-proceedings suplemental materials that covered the same subject. i am really pushing for the reader to see this is a computational paradigm not just another document." .

rul:R-44 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Page title A nested lifecycle (sentence case as the site's titles are); its specification block now says which items each cycle contributes to the one record, that coverage and the traceback read both sides in one query, and that S0-Layers and M4-Nesting guarantee it; the walkthrough's layer table is introduced as that count." ;
    ogc:order 44 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-50 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The page title The nested model becomes A Nested Lifecycle: model is a throwaway word for most readers and should not trip anyone up. The nested lifecycle page must also address the fact that the records and reporting rely on information produced during both the contracting lifecycle and the evaluation lifecycle." ;
    ogc:verbatim "\"the nested model\" should be \"A Nested Lifecycle\". \"model\" is a throwaway word for most people and we don't want to trip anyone up. we also need to make sure that the page on the nested lifecycle addresses the fact that the records and reporting rely on information produced during BOTH the contracting lifecycle and the evaluation lifecycle." .

rul:R-45 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Appendix D says OpenSysML is the modelling tool, used at authoring time to validate and to render the model to RDF, fetched by digest outside the lockfile, with everything downstream reading only the RDF; and gains an ontologies section, a register of every namespace the committed graphs use (W3C RDF, RDFS, OWL, XSD, SKOS, PROV-O, EARL, SHACL; OMG SysML v2 as OpenSysML renders it and its tool facts; the specification's own namespaces) with the classes, properties and subjects each contributes, counted from the files; a namespace in use but not registered fails the test." ;
    ogc:order 45 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-51 ;
    ogc:rulingText "OpenSysML is absent from the runtime environment because it was used only for modelling, through the features that render the models to RDF. The toolchain review therefore needs a section on ontologies, addressing PROV-O, EARL, SysML and the others." ;
    ogc:verbatim "why isn't opensysml in the runtime environement? i guess we only used it for modeling since we used the features that render the models to rdf. i think this means we need a section on ontologies in the toolchain to address provo-o, earl, sysml, and more" .

rul:R-46 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "The layers poster is retired from the registry. The view nesting draws the outer chain with fulfil as a black box, the inner chain it opens into, and only the flows that cross the boundary, bundled by item kind and read from the model graph (the outer flows into the process's parameters and the binds that hand them to inner steps); the page says what it leaves out. The rule joins the views doctrine: a view communicates one thing; black-box the rest; when wiring two systems, draw the interface." ;
    ogc:order 46 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-52 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The layers diagram is illegible to a human and needs the treatment given to the earlier diagrams. Views are designed to communicate clearly as views, not to be exhaustive; this may require several diagrams, or it may be as simple as black-boxing subsystems more effectively. Wiring two systems together often means focusing on how they interface with each other, even when the interfaces span scales." ;
    ogc:verbatim "more feedback. this diagram is human illegible. needs similiar treatment to earlier diagrams. views need to be designed to communicate clearly as views not to be exhaustive. this means we may need multiple diagrams or it may be as simple as blackboxing subsystems more effectively. wiring two systems together often involves focusing on how they interface with each other, even when the interfaces span scales." .

rul:R-47 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "06-14: the thirteen quotes ticked (human, Z, 2026-09-06); none pending. 06-06: outcome's canonical is SEVOCAB test result, EARL a seeAlso and the binding. 06-07: NIST TN 1297 rank reserve. 06-08: six fragment quotes say so in their locators. 06-10: the four coined terms cite nothing; they carry ogc:coinedBy; the internal sources leave the register. 06-02: the crosswalk cites the public SciPy 2026 session (src:scipy-2026-bof, digest) instead of the deck; 06-11: Popper 1959 stands alongside every row (ogc:also). 06-01, 06-03, 06-05: the front page and the chapters restructured (prose and headings, judicious info blocks, code blocks that pull content, terms defined at first use). 06-04: the rulings rewritten in a formal register with the verbatim messages kept in the graph as ogc:verbatim. 06-09: process canon researched for C2, C4, C6 and step 2 and re-cited. 06-12: ogc reads the record. 06-13: ogc execute exposes the executor's parameters. 06-15: Appendix D's descriptions rendered from the commands' own output." ;
    ogc:order 47 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-44,
        rul:C-53 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Round one of the simulated user test is adjudicated item by item (sheet 06). The front page opens in three sentences and names the measles case early. The bridge cites the public Birds-of-a-Feather session at SciPy 2026 as a fact on the record rather than a private artefact; the forthcoming blog post may be added later. The chapters are reorganized: prose with headings that shape the outline, information blocks used sparingly, and code blocks that demonstrably pull content from the model, in the manner of the vendor fraud example. The rulings are rewritten in a formal register with their intent preserved and the message as sent kept in the graph. Terms are defined at first use, as the SciPy proceedings reviewers approved. The canonical source of outcome is SEVOCAB's test result; NIST TN 1297 is a reserve source; fragment quotes say so; Popper 1959 stands alongside every crosswalk row. The four weak step matches are re-cited from process canon, since the posture is implementation of standards and not their re-litigation, the contribution being the application to contextual AI evaluation and the executable specification. The coined terms cite nothing and are owned. ogc reads the record and exposes the executor's parameters. The thirteen pending quotes are ticked. Appendix D is aligned with the code it describes." ;
    ogc:verbatim "rulings: 6-1: a as recommended. 6-2: don't cite the deck, site the public event. the BoF at SciPy 2026 is on the public record. you can reference it as a fact about events and what transpired there rather than a citation to a private artifact. there is a blog going live on the hi site soon about it which we can add as an additional citation later. 6-3: reorganize to make more judicious use of info blocks. balance info blocks with standard prose (and headers effecting outline) and code blocks which demonstrable pull content (see techniques in the vendor fraud example). 6-4: rework the rulings content so it doesn't read as messages i sent to an assistant. the intents should be preserved but the tone should shift to a more formal register. 6-5: define terms on first use. you can look at the conversations around the scipy-proceeding paper i wrote. look at the discussions with the reviewer and the fixes we made and they approved for guidance. 6-6: a as recommended. 6-7: a as recommended. 6-8: a as recommended. 6-9: dig in deeper to make sure we align with process canon. shouldn't seem like a stretch. our posture is implementation of standards not relitagating them. our contribution is application to contextual AI eval domain and executable spec implementation not standards authoring. 6-10: drop citations and own the coinage. 6-11: a as recommended. 6-12: a as recommended. 6-13: a as recommended. 6-14: tick them. 6-15: align the code and the appendix more clearly" .

rul:R-50 a ogc:Ruling ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-07"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    ogc:changeNote "Sheet 10's ruling column; ticks in rulings/validated.ttl; the register's ISO 9000 status and SEVOCAB retrieval note; the works cited author order. The model, ontology, shape, record and chapter changes the rulings call for follow in slices." ;
    ogc:order 50 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-56 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Round three (sheet 10) is adjudicated item by item, and sheet 05 read one row at a time. Contract: the sponsor approves the requirement set (10-01); the report is the record's delivered export; the record is auditable and reproducible but delivered only as a special case (10-02); acceptance is the sponsor's act on receipt, recognizing completion, for which a correctly constructed record is necessary and not sufficient (10-03); the access grant carries the version identity, the period and the instrument (10-04); the agreement accepts the proposal and the statement of work, the access and the delivery are under the agreement (10-05); the sponsor has a signatory person (10-06); independence and user interest are declaration items (10-07); 17000's own reading of party holds, the sponsor second-party, the independent testing organization third-party, the co-incidence cases named (10-08); the test item provider is accountable for the test item as its provider, the predicate is providesTestItem, and the sponsor's obligation to the affected populations is not transferred (10-09). Evaluation: minPassRate is dropped for this version and weights carry a rationale and a timestamp (10-10); the report approval covers the recommendation, which rests on every attestation the coverage used or names its exclusions (10-11); appropriateness covers the assumptions and the test planning, and sufficiency relates the evidence to the claim: insufficient evidence implies cannot tell, sufficient evidence gives pass or fail (10-12); a person holds at most one actor role and any team member may represent a population (10-13); no cherry-picking: a determination is justified against all relevant evidence in the record (10-14); S5 mirrors M5 and the record states its independence level (10-15); deviations from the plan are items (10-16); the test item is bound across the record and records are linked by revision (10-17); the verdict and the coverage computation carry digests of the shapes, the ontology and the query (10-18); the ordering rules, the recomputed rates and the computed verdict are accepted as built (10-19); test driver stays, human and machine work complementary, separated in the test plan a human deems appropriate (10-20). Standards: 17000 4.10 leaves C4 for C2, with Annex A A.2 on C4 (10-21); the outer cycle names its canon per step and is performed under an agreement (10-22); attestation is refined, attesting passed, failed or cannot tell on specific evidence with supersession by later attestations (10-23); determination is refined as 17000's decision (10-24); technical expert is refined: not all technical experts attest, every attestation comes from a relevant role (10-25); the evaluation operator is anchored to tester (10-26); authorized representatives exist per organization and no canonical definition is read reversed (10-27); ISO 9000:2026 stays a preview, checked 2026-09-07 (10-28); the SEVOCAB statement is recorded as printed (10-29); mission's obligations towards the affected populations are marked as the specification's facets of the organization's purpose (10-30). Graphs: the record becomes a PROV bundle now (10-31); the register and the model vocabularies are declared with headers (10-32); the term map is realized in the model graph and the step derived from it now (10-33); the PROV subproperty on actsOnBehalfOf is dropped (10-34); the w3id namespace is assumed to merge (10-35); report and recommendation become terms and the machines and roles one paragraph (10-36); the retired word stays paraphrased (10-37); the site carries its version and commit (10-38); the gate prints a failing step's cause (10-39); sources carry BibTeX keys and shapes their counterexamples (10-40); the conformance check is on the record and precedes the final report, a draft report may carry flagged gaps and a final report cannot, and the report assembler is rebuilt so (10-41). Sheet 05: B3, B9, B11, B12 and the wires W22, W30, W32, W34, W35, W36, W39 are validated; B8 awaits its rebuild, and concern C-30 narrows to it. The works cited name Hollek and Zargham for the SciPy 2026 session, Hollek first. The worked example is the measles evaluation, not the measles run: neither word is a term, run reads at the execution scale and case at the test-case scale, and the whole is one Contextual AI Evaluation; its namespace is renamed accordingly (10-42). Synthetic content is tagged as such in the graph and the explorer can include or exclude it (10-43)." ;
    ogc:verbatim """10-01: (a) Sponsor approves the requirement set (Recommended)
10-02: "the report is the delivered export from the record. the full record is not delivered but the report is an export from the record that fulfills the obligations of the contract. the full record is independently auditable and the methods are reproducible. however access to the full record rather than the exported report (which may be an interactive dashboard) is a special case. there may be special cases where the full record is published but that is not assumed."
10-03: "strictly speaking the sponsor will review and accept their deliverables per standard practice. this is a contract loop activity that recognizes the competion of the contracts obligations. correctly constructed record is necessary but not sufficient for acceptance because acceptance is an act reserved for the sponsor upon reciept of the deliverables"
10-04: (a) Version, period, instrument; environment as text (Recommended)
10-28: (b) still a preview, checked 2026-09-07. 10-29: statement as printed (screenshot), 2026-09-07.
10-05: (a) Link them, keep the statement of work at C3 (Recommended)
10-06: (a) A sponsor signatory person (Recommended)
10-07: (a) Two declaration items (Recommended)
10-08: (a) The standard's reading (Recommended)
10-09: (a) providesTestItem, and the obligation is not transferred (Recommended)
10-10: (a) Drop minPassRate; rationale and timestamps on weights (Recommended)
10-11: (a) The approval covers the recommendation; all attestations or named exclusions (Recommended)
10-12: "appropriateness is used to cover assumptions and test planning; it doesn't matter how much evidence you collect if your plan or strategy and associated assumptions are not appropriate for what they aim to determine. 

sufficiency relates evidence backing up a claim that a requirement is met or not. insufficient evidence implies decision is cannot tell. we can have sufficient evidence resulting in either a pass or fail."
10-13: (a) S0-Roles; any team member may represent (Recommended)
10-14: "no cherry picking you need to justify your determination accounting for all relevant evidence in the record. 

i am unclear on what a mutation is since in principle the record is append only"
10-15: (a) S5 mirrors M5, and an independence level on the record (Recommended)
10-16: (a) PlanDeviation items, required by shape (Recommended)
10-17: (a) Bind the test item across the record; revisions between records (Recommended)
10-18: (a) Digests on the verdict and the coverage computation (Recommended)
10-20: "operator is responsible for the human roles in the test plan but there is machine work and a test driver does drive, the human and machine aspects are assumed to be complementary and the record always tracks who did what but we don't explicitly privilege the human or the machine a priori; exact separation of responsibility lives in the test plan. a human must deem the test plan appropriate for what its testing"
10-21 (first pass): "i need further clarification, i am not clear on what we mean by applicant"
10-21 (clarified: applicant = the sponsor asking for the service): (a) Drop 4.10 from C4; keep it on C2; cite Annex A A.2 Selection on C4 (Recommended)
10-22: (a) Name the actual canon per step; performed under an agreement (Recommended)
10-23: "scope note states the extension and we're explicitly using EARL so you can attest pass/fail/cannot tell. these are conditioned on specific evidence, new attestations can supercede old attestations if more evidence is secured"
10-24: (a) Determination refined, with the shift stated (Recommended)
10-25: "not all technical experts attest but all attestation must come from relevant roles, in this case a technical expert."
10-26: (a) SEVOCAB tester if the export defines it, else NIST AI 700-2 Tester; 12207 operator as seeAlso (Recommended)
10-27: "there are multiple authorized representatives since they exist on a per organization basis; avoid reversal of canonical definitions"
10-30: "you can mark the addition but be clear that these obligations toward affected populations would come from the organizations purpose. its not a separate idea, its drawing out the relevant facets"
10-31: (b) The bundle now
10-32: (a) register.ttl and ogm.ttl with headers (Recommended)
10-33: "realize the term map now and derive step now"
10-34: (a) Drop the subproperty axiom, keep a plain epo property (Recommended)
10-35: "i made the pull request to w3id for the og-caie namespace. it hasn't merged yet but can we work under the assumption it will be"
10-36: (a) Entries for report and recommendation; the machines and roles in one paragraph (Recommended)
10-38: (a) Version and commit in the front-page footer; CITATION.cff at v0.3.0 when tagged (Recommended)
10-40: (a) ogc:bibkey on sources and ogc:counterexample on shapes (Recommended)
10-42 (Z, mid-walkthrough): "measles run" feels off to me. i feel like the term "Case" is more appropriate than "Run" for what we're describing. can you do a canon/glossary check and help me decide -> after the check: "evaluation": the measles evaluation (Recommended)
10-43 (Z): i also think given that our models cases are synthetic we need to be able to tag synthetic content as such and have a filter toggle that includes/excludes synthetic data in the graph viewer
The works cited (Z, earlier on 2026-09-07): citation 20 "Zargham, & Hollek. (2026). Building Scientific Approaches to Generative AI. Birds-of-a-Feather session, SciPy 2026." should be Hollek and Zargham. reverse the author order for that one. Julie was first author
B11 TestDriver: Validated
W22 probeRecordSeam: "probes by definition occur as part of test plans and the probe and its measured response both must be captured as part of the record. though often the individual probe-response measurement pairs will be part of larger data sets which will be analyzed in order to make determinations. that said i want you to be very careful about mixing abstractions probes and measured responses are the smallest units of data we account for and we leave them intentionally abstract"
W22: Validated (on the abstraction understanding). W34 responseSeam: Validated
B12 TestItem: Validated
B8 ReportAssembler (NOT ticked; reorder ruled): "the record is what has a conformance check. conformance is a prerequisite for compiling the final report. a draft report can be produced even if it has gaps in traceability or coverage but those gaps must be flagged and the report cannot be final with gaps because it will fail on conformance according to our correct by construction rule that uses shacl to check bidirectional traceability and coverage"
W39: Validated as the wire; the verdict's subject becomes the record. W30: Validated""" .

src:w3c-skos a prov:Entity,
